<Docs
	page="recipes/javascript"
	title="JavaScript & TypeScript"
	lede="node-postgres, better-sqlite3, bun:sqlite, postgres.js, and Prisma's raw queries work out of the box."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Template literals (<code>`&hellip;`</code>) take newlines as written, so a
			query that outgrows its line can spread over several. Strings in quotes,{" "}
			<code>"&hellip;"</code> or <code>'&hellip;'</code>, are left exactly as
			written &mdash; to have squill format a query, write it as a template
			literal. Passed to <code>.query</code>, a template with a{" "}
			<code>$&lbrace;&hellip;&rbrace;</code> hole pastes text into the SQL, so
			it&rsquo;s left alone; a tagged template whose library sends the hole as a
			parameter is formatted around it (below).
		</p>
		<p>
			Each language is its own grammar: <code>javascript</code> (which includes
			JSX), <code>typescript</code>, and <code>tsx</code>. A project with more
			than one needs a rule for each.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>node-postgres</h2>
		<p>
			The default query finds the first string or template argument of{" "}
			<code>query</code>, <code>execute</code>, and <code>prepare</code> method
			calls on any object, which covers pg&rsquo;s <code>Pool</code> and{" "}
			<code>Client</code>. No query file needed:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="js-pg" lang="typescript" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>better-sqlite3 and bun:sqlite</h2>
		<p>
			The same methods, with the dialect set to SQLite, which is also what makes{" "}
			<code>?</code>, <code>@name</code>, and <code>$name</code> placeholders
			parse. bun:sqlite&rsquo;s <code>db.query()</code> is found the same way as{" "}
			<code>db.prepare()</code>:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="js-sqlite" lang="typescript" />
		<p>
			<code>db.exec()</code>, for migrations and other multi-statement scripts,
			isn&rsquo;t in the default query; a query of your own can add{" "}
			<code>"exec"</code> to the method names.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>postgres.js and Bun.sql</h2>
		<p>
			The default query also finds <code>sql`&hellip;`</code> tagged templates,
			and Prisma&rsquo;s <code>$queryRaw`&hellip;`</code> and{" "}
			<code>$executeRaw`&hellip;`</code>. Those send each{" "}
			<code>$&lbrace;&hellip;&rbrace;</code> hole as a parameter, so squill
			reads it as one, formats around it, and writes it back as it was. A
			fragment nested in a hole (<code>$&lbrace;sql`&hellip;`&rbrace;</code>) is
			part of the query it&rsquo;s in, and left alone; a fragment on its own
			isn&rsquo;t a whole statement, and is reported as SQL that doesn&rsquo;t
			parse.
		</p>
		<Recipe name="js-postgres" lang="typescript" />
	</section>
</Docs>
